  <!-- Section 9: FAQ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Tech Pack FAQ</h2>
      
      <div class="faq">
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Do I need a tech pack to work with a clothing manufacturer?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Yes, you need a tech pack to work with most clothing manufacturers. Without a tech pack, manufacturers must guess your specifications, which leads to incorrect sizing, wrong fabric choices, and costly sample revisions. A tech pack provides the technical blueprint needed for accurate production. Some manufacturers offer tech pack creation services if you don't have one.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What happens if I don't have a tech pack?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Without a tech pack, manufacturers will either refuse the project or charge significantly more to create one for you. Production delays are common as factories must clarify ambiguous specifications. Sample costs increase as multiple iterations are needed to get the product right. The final product may not match your vision, leading to costly rework or unsellable inventory.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How much does it cost to create a tech pack?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Tech pack costs vary by method: DIY with software costs $0 (if you own Adobe Illustrator) but takes 5-10 hours per style with a steep learning curve. Freelance designers charge $200-500 per style for simple garments and $500-1,500 for complex styles. Tech pack agencies charge $300-800 per style. SDF Clothing includes tech pack creation in our full package quote for clients who don't have one.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Can SDF create a tech pack from my design sketches?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Yes, SDF Clothing can create a tech pack from your design sketches. Our design team converts creative sketches into technical manufacturing documents. We review your sketches, clarify specifications, and produce complete tech packs ready for production. This service is included in our full package quote or available as a standalone service for brands who need tech pack creation.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What software do designers use to create tech packs?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Professional designers use Adobe Illustrator for tech pack creation due to its precision and industry-standard vector capabilities. Dedicated platforms like Techpacker.com offer collaborative features for tech pack management. Canva can be used for basic tech packs but lacks technical precision. Excel with manual drawings is possible but inefficient. Most fashion brands hire freelance designers with Illustrator expertise rather than learning the software themselves.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How long does it take to create a tech pack?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Tech pack creation time varies by experience and complexity. Experienced designers take 2-4 hours for simple garments like t-shirts and 4-8 hours for complex garments like jackets. Beginners may take 10-20 hours per style due to the learning curve. Freelance designers typically deliver within 3-5 business days. SDF Clothing delivers tech packs within 5-7 business days as part of our development process.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is the difference between a tech pack and a spec sheet?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            A tech pack is a comprehensive document including sketches, measurements, fabric specifications, color callouts, trim details, construction notes, and quality standards. A spec sheet is typically a subset focusing only on measurements and basic specifications. Tech packs are more detailed and used for full production, while spec sheets may be used for simple sampling or quick costing. Professional manufacturing requires full tech packs for accuracy.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Can I update a tech pack after production starts?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">
            Yes, you can update a tech pack after production starts, but changes incur costs and delays. Minor changes like adjusting measurements within tolerance may be possible without significant impact. Major changes like fabric substitution or style modification require re-sampling and may incur additional charges. It's best to finalize your tech pack before bulk production to avoid costly revisions. SDF Clothing reviews tech packs before production to catch issues early.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

